# portal 1.03 patch 24 — the updater points at this project
#
# `const repository = 'pluralplay/FlClashX'` and asset names `FlClashX-android-$arch.apk`,
# `FlClashX-windows-$arch-setup.exe`, `FlClashX-macos-$arch.dmg`,
# `FlClashX-linux-$arch.{AppImage,deb,rpm}`: the updater asked another project's repository for
# another project's files, so a check either found nothing or offered a build that is not this
# app.
#
# Now the repository is `saved-j/portal` and the names are this project's own:
#
#   android arm64-v8a   Portal.apk
#   android armeabi-v7a Portal_legacy.apk
#   android x86_64      Portal_x86-64.apk
#   android universal   Portal_universal.apk
#   windows             Portal.exe
#   macos               Portal.dmg
#   linux               Portal-*.AppImage | Portal-*.deb | Portal-*.rpm
#
# `build.sh apk` writes those four Android builds into `dist/` as `Portal-android-<abi>.apk`
# today, so those four names are accepted as well, after the published ones — this project's
# files under another spelling, not another project's file.
#
# The Linux packages carry a version in the name, so they are matched against the release's own
# `assets` list (and a name that states another arch is still refused). Everything else is an
# exact name in that list: UpdateTarget.pick() searches the release, it does not build a URL
# from a guess.
#
# A release that carries nothing for this platform/arch now says so, with the names it looked
# for (`noAsset` with detail `Portal.apk | Portal_universal.apk`), and the dialog shows that
# detail; no FlClashX file is ever offered in its place. The download logs both facts:
#
#   [APP] update: repository=saved-j/portal asset=Portal.apk url=https://github.com/...
diff --git a/BUILD.md b/BUILD.md
index 80c4cee0..96c26655 100644
--- a/BUILD.md
+++ b/BUILD.md
@@ -232,6 +232,36 @@ portal delay Финляндия: fi.example:443 → 185.155.222.6 214 ms
 
 `adb logcat -s flutter` carries those lines; the dash on the row and the line are the same fact.
 
+## Updates
+
+The updater reads `saved-j/portal` (`repository` in `lib/common/constant.dart`) and looks only
+for the names this project publishes:
+
+| platform | install kind | asset |
+|---|---|---|
+| Android arm64 | `UpdateInstallKind.android` | `Portal.apk` |
+| Android arm32 | same, `armeabi-v7a` | `Portal_legacy.apk` |
+| Android x86_64 | same, `x86_64` | `Portal_x86-64.apk` |
+| Android, any ABI's fallback | same, `universal` | `Portal_universal.apk` |
+| Windows | `windows` | `Portal.exe` |
+| macOS | `macos` | `Portal.dmg` |
+| Linux | `appImage` / `deb` / `rpm` | `Portal-*.AppImage` / `Portal-*.deb` / `Portal-*.rpm` |
+
+`./build.sh apk` writes `dist/Portal-android-<abi>.apk` today; the updater accepts those four
+names too (`portalAndroidDistNames`), after the published ones. Nothing from another project is
+accepted under any name.
+
+The Linux names carry a version, so the release's own `assets` list decides which one it
+published; an arch token in the name (`linux-arm64`, `linux-amd64`) still has to be this
+device's. A release that carries nothing for this platform/arch is reported as
+`updateErrorNoAsset` with the names it looked for, e.g. `This release has no build for this
+device.` over `Portal.apk | Portal_universal.apk` — never another project's file. The download
+line names both facts:
+
+```
+[APP] update: repository=saved-j/portal asset=Portal.apk url=https://github.com/saved-j/portal/releases/download/v1.0.3/Portal.apk
+```
+
